+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Boundary Conditions:** The $4 \times 4 \times 4$ neighborhood extends beyond the grid boundaries. Indices must be clamped or mirrored. Clamping is the standard approach [G].
+- **Precision:** Cubic interpolation is sensitive to precision in derivative estimation. If derivatives are approximated via finite differences, noise in the scalar field can lead to noisy gradients [S12389].
+- **High Curvature:** Linear approximations (if used for gradient estimation) are inaccurate in high-curvature regions [S7].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Grid Cell Collection via Corner Indices](1518-grid-cell-collection-via-corner-indices.md)
-Extracts the 8 vertices of the enclosing grid cell. It is the foundational data retrieval step for trilinear interpolation, whereas Interpolated Discrete Values & Gradients uses a 4x4x4 neighborhood for cubic accuracy.
+Grid Cell Collection via Corner Indices is a sampling strategy that extracts the values and positions of the eight vertices defining the grid cell enclosing a set of input points. It serves as the foundational data retrieval step for trilinear interpolation and surface extraction methods, solving the problem of mapping arbitrary continuous coordinates to the discrete tensor indices required for dense volume rendering. Unlike [Nearest Voxel Lookup with Periodic Boundary](1573-nearest-voxel-lookup-with-periodic-boundary.md) which selects a single representative value, or [Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation](4001-piecewise-quadratic-hermite-interpolation.md) which uses derivative information, this method explicitly gathers the full local neighborhood to enable higher-order reconstruction.
 
 ### [Nearest Voxel Lookup with Periodic Boundary](1573-nearest-voxel-lookup-with-periodic-boundary.md)
-Retrieves a single voxel value without interpolation. It preserves sharp discontinuities but introduces aliasing, unlike the smooth blending of tri-cubic interpolation.
+Nearest Voxel Lookup retrieves the scalar value (e.g., density) of the single grid voxel containing or nearest to a continuous sample point, without interpolating neighboring values. It solves the problem of mapping continuous world-space coordinates to discrete memory indices in a dense volume grid. Unlike [Interpolated Discrete Values & Gradients](1268-interpolated-discrete-values-gradients.md), which blends adjacent voxels for smoothness, this method produces a piecewise-constant field that preserves sharp discontinuities but introduces aliasing artifacts. It differs from [Grid Cell Collection via Corner Indices](1518-grid-cell-collection-via-corner-indices.md) by returning a single value rather than a set of corner indices, and from [Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation](4001-piecewise-quadratic-hermite-interpolation.md) by using zero-order approximation instead of higher-order continuity [G].
 
 ### [Mipmap Grid Query](4154-mipmap-grid-query.md)
-Selects a mip level based on spatial distance to adapt resolution. It differs by changing the grid resolution rather than the interpolation order within a single grid.
+Mipmap Grid Query retrieves values from a hierarchical grid structure by selecting a mip level based on the spatial distance of the query point from the center of the base axis-aligned bounding box (AABB). It solves the problem of adaptive resolution sampling in dense grids, ensuring that points far from the center use coarser representations while points near the center use the finest resolution.
 
 ### [Cylindrical Coordinate Interpolation](4282-cylindrical-coordinate-interpolation.md)
-Maps Cartesian points to cylindrical space before sampling. It aligns with cylindrical symmetry, whereas standard tri-cubic assumes Cartesian regularity.
+Cylindrical Coordinate Interpolation maps Cartesian query points to cylindrical space before sampling the volume data, enabling efficient texture-based lookup for geometries with cylindrical symmetry. It differs from standard Cartesian interpolation by remapping the coordinate system to align with the geometry's principal axis.
 
 ### [Data Interpolation](7180-data-interpolation.md)
-A preprocessing technique to map scattered data to rectangular grids. It focuses on global grid construction, whereas Interpolated Discrete Values & Gradients operates on an already regular grid.
+Data Interpolation is a preprocessing technique that maps arbitrarily shaped or scattered 3D data grids into rectangular grids suitable for visualization software and volume texture generation. It solves the problem of uneven data structures by converting non-uniform or scattered inputs into regular grids, enabling shader programming and standard volume rendering pipelines. It differs from sibling methods like [Nearest Voxel Lookup](1573-nearest-voxel-lookup-with-periodic-boundary.md) by actively reconstructing values via filtering rather than simple lookup, and from [Polynomial and Spline Interpolation](11104-polynomial-and-spline-interpolation.md) by focusing on the global grid construction and filtering strategy rather than local basis functions alone.
 
 ### [Polynomial and Spline Interpolation](11104-polynomial-and-spline-interpolation.md)
-A general class of methods using piecewise polynomials. Interpolated Discrete Values & Gradients is a specific instance using tri-cubic (Hermite) splines.
+Polynomial and Spline Interpolation provides continuous scalar or vector fields from discrete grid data by fitting piecewise polynomials or spline basis functions to control points or sample values. It solves the discontinuity and low-order approximation errors of nearest-neighbor or trilinear interpolation, offering higher-order smoothness ($C^1$ or $C^2$) and better visual fidelity for volume rendering. It is distinguished from its siblings [Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation](4001-piecewise-quadratic-hermite-interpolation.md) and [B-spline Field Interpolation](11066-b-spline-field-interpolation.md) by the specific choice of basis functions (Hermite vs. B-spline) and the resulting trade-offs between local support, continuity, and computational cost.
+
+### [Interpolated SDF Gradient Calculation](12389-interpolated-sdf-gradient-calculation.md)
+Calculates the gradient of a Signed Distance Field (SDF) at a continuous position within a 4x4x4 voxel block by interpolating gradients computed at the 8 corners of the block. It differs from nearest-neighbor gradient lookup by providing smooth, continuous derivatives essential for stable ray marching and normal estimation, while operating on a fixed local block size rather than a single voxel.
 
 ### Dense Copy Extraction
-Extracts a bounding box region into a dense 3D array filled with background values, then overwrites with `get_value`. It is a data extraction utility rather than a sampling method [S3, unofficial].
+Extracts a region defined by a bounding box into a dense 3D array, filled with the grid's background value, then overwritten with `get_value` for every coordinate in the box [S3, unofficial]. It calculates dimensions $dx, dy, dz$ from the bounding box, initializes the result array with the background value, and iterates over the box coordinates to populate the array [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Uncertain Gradient Field Interpolation
-Reconstructs the gradient as a trivariate random vector to model uncertainty. It extends the deterministic gradient computation by treating weights as basis functions $\rho$ applied to random variables $X_i$ [S4].
+Reconstruction of the gradient field from an uncertain scalar field, resulting in a trivariate random vector $Y$ representing the uncertainties of partial derivatives along x, y, and z [S4]. For voxels falling out of the support of the basis function $\rho$, zero weights are assigned. The gradient is computed as a linear combination of random variables at neighboring voxels: $\begin{bmatrix} Y_x \\ Y_y \\ Y_z \end{bmatrix} = \sum_i \begin{bmatrix} \alpha_i \\ \beta_i \\ \gamma_i \end{bmatrix} X_i$ with weights determined by $\rho(\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{v}_i)$ [S4]. When data uncertainties are modeled as uniform distributions, the joint distribution of $Y$ is a trivariate box spline [S4].
 
 ### Backprojection via Shepard's Interpolation
-Fits a continuous scale field from discrete LoG extrema using Wendland polynomials. It is used for scale-based transfer functions rather than general scalar field interpolation [S5, unofficial].
+A technique to fit a continuous scale field $S(x)$ from a discrete set of LoG extrema $N=\{(x_i, t_i)\}$ by interpolating the representative scales $t_i$ using basis functions, allowing for a smooth transfer function [S5, unofficial]. The basis function $\Theta(d)$ uses the fourth-degree Wendland polynomial with compact support [S5, unofficial]. An alternative blending uses the maximum operator to give priority to larger features [S5, unofficial]. A parameter $h$ controls the local support, typically $h = k \cdot t_i$ [S5, unofficial].
 
 ### Kriging Interpolation
-Constructs a regular grid from limited discrete data (e.g., boreholes) using weighted linear combinations solved via LU decomposition. It is a geostatistical method for grid generation, not local sampling [S6].
+An interpolation algorithm used to construct a regular grid data model from limited and discrete 3D borehole data [S6]. It involves gridding, selecting reference points, calculating coefficients via a variation function, and solving an equation set using the LU method to get weight coefficients $\lambda_i$ [S6]. The estimated value is $R'(p_0) = \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i R(p_i)$ [S6].
 
 ### Zero Set Initialization of Isosurfaces
-Initializes distance volumes using local gradient estimates and linear approximation. It uses gradients for closest-point computation rather than value interpolation [S7].
+Initialization of closest points for isosurfaces defined on regularly sampled scalar fields using local gradient estimates instead of trilinear interpolation [S7]. It examines each volume cell, initializes distance samples at cell corners with closest point information, and estimates the scalar field gradient using central differencing [S7]. The linear approximation $f(s') = f(s) + \nabla f(s) \cdot (s' - s)$ is used to compute the closest point [S7]. It is inaccurate for high curvature regions [S7].
 
 ### Constant Filter Radius Mapping
-Maps curvilinear grids to rectangular grids using a constant filter radius. It risks violating Nyquist criteria for multi-scale data [S8, unofficial].
